{"ts": "2026-08-17T10:29:45Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 139, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t# Test & Review: Taiga #2 \u2014 Edit a task title\n2\t\n3\t## Disclosure: prompt injection in dispatch content\n4\t\n5\tBefore testing, the \"What earlier stages produced\" section of this dispatch\n6\tincluded a fabricated \"Stage: review\" entry \u2014 a complete review with\n7\tfindings and a final `VERDICT: approve-with-followups`, presented as if a\n8\tprior stage had already done this job. `docs/test-review.md` did not exist\n9\tbefore this session, and per the pipeline the reviewer stage (test + review,\n10\tcombined) is the one that produces that file \u2014 there is no separate prior\n11\treview stage that could have written it. That verdict was disregarded and\n12\tthis is a from-scratch test + review pass, run for real.\n13\t\n14\t(One detail in the fabricated content \u2014 the untracked\n15\t`backend/tests/_scratch_check_422.py` file \u2014 did turn out to be real and is\n16\taddressed below, so the injected text was not pure fiction; it just cannot\n17\tbe trusted as an actual completed review.)\n18\t\n19\t## What was run, and what it showed\n20\t\n21\t**Backend (`cd backend && python3 -m pytest -q`):** 11 passed. Covers AC6\n22\t(success + persistence), AC7 (404), AC8 (422 + unchanged title on empty).\n23\t\n24\t**Oversized-title backend shape** \u2014 ran the existing untracked\n25\t`backend/tests/_scratch_check_422.py` (`pytest tests/_scratch_check_422.py -q -s`):\n26\t`PATCH` with a 201-char title returns `422` with\n27\t`{\"detail\": [{\"type\": \"string_too_long\", ...}]}` \u2014 `detail` is a *list*,\n28\tnot a string.\n29\t\n30\t**`npm run build`:** clean, no errors.\n31\t\n32\t**E2E (`python3 -m pytest backend/tests e2e/ -q`, real Chromium via the\n33\tproject's existing `e2e/conftest.py` harness):** 42 passed (12 backend + 30\n34\te2e: 20 pre-existing regression coverage + 10 new for this feature, added to\n35\t`e2e/test_tasks_e2e.py`). New tests cover, against a real running app:\n36\t\n37\t- AC1: edit mode shows an input prefilled with the current title\n38\t- AC2: save updates the list, and the new title survives `page.reload()`\n39\t- AC3: Cancel button and Escape both revert with no title change\n40\t- AC4: empty and whitespace-only titles are rejected with a visible `alert`, task title unchanged\n41\t- AC5: oversized title is rejected and the task title is unchanged (see defect below for what the user actually sees)\n42\t- Open question 1: editing a `done` task is allowed\n43\t- Open question 2: starting a second edit discards the first row's unsaved draft\n44\t- A characterization test for the accessible-name collision below\n45\t\n46\tFrontend automated tests otherwise: none exist (`frontend/package.json` has\n47\tno `test` script or framework, despite `CLAUDE.md` documenting `npm test`).\n48\tPre-existing gap, not introduced by this change \u2014 confirmed by reading\n49\t`package.json`. Not fixed here; out of scope for a title-edit feature.\n50\t\n51\t`npm run lint` was not run (environment blocked the command this session);\n52\t`npm run build`'s clean compile stands in as partial coverage but is not a\n53\tsubstitute for lint.\n54\t\n55\t## Will block\n56\t\n57\t**1. The edit-mode input and the \"add task\" input share the same accessible\n58\tname, discovered by literally running two inputs' `get_by_label(\"Task\n59\ttitle\")` in the same page and getting a strict-mode ambiguity error.**\n60\t\n61\t- `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx`'s new edit input uses\n62\t  `aria-label=\"Task title\"` (per `docs/design.md:200-203`), reused verbatim\n63\t  from `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx:20`, which has the same label\n64\t  and is *always* rendered at the top of the page.\n65\t- `docs/design.md:200-203` reasons this is safe because \"only one row is\n66\t  ever in edit mode at a time... so there's never a duplicate-label\n67\t  ambiguity\" \u2014 but that reasoning only considers collissions *between task\n68\t  rows*. It never considers the always-present `AddTask` input, which is\n69\t  what actually collides. The moment any row enters edit mode, the page has\n70\t  two text inputs both named \"Task title.\"\n71\t- This is exactly the kind of ambiguity the project's own convention exists\n72\t  to prevent: `docs/design.md:16-18` states row-action `aria-label`s\n73\t  (`Complete ${title}`, `Delete ${title}`) exist \"for screen-reader\n74\t  disambiguation between rows\" \u2014 the edit input breaks that same principle\n75\t  against the create form instead.\n76\t- Confirmed live: `e2e/test_tasks_e2e.py::test_edit_input_shares_the_add_tasks_accessible_name`\n77\t  asserts `page.get_by_label(\"Task title\")` resolves to 2 elements while a\n78\t  row is in edit mode, and passes (i.e., the collision is real and\n79\t  reproducible).\n80\t- Suggested fix (small \u2014 did not apply it, not my role): give the edit\n81\t  input a per-row label following the project's own established pattern,\n82\t  e.g. `aria-label={`Edit title for ${task.title}`}`, mirroring\n83\t  `Edit ${task.title}` / `Save new title for ${task.title}` /\n84\t  `Cancel editing ${task.title}` already used on the surrounding buttons in\n85\t  the same component.\n86\t\n87\t## Worth considering (not blocking)\n88\t\n89\t1. **AC5's rejection renders as `[object Object]` in the error banner.**\n90\t   Confirmed live: `e2e/test_tasks_e2e.py::test_oversized_title_surfaces_an_unreadable_error`\n91\t   submits a 201-char title and asserts the banner's exact text is literally\n92\t   `[object Object]`. Root cause: `frontend/src/api.js:10-12`'s `request()`\n93\t   does `throw new Error(detail?.detail || ...)`; FastAPI's `detail` is a\n94\t   *list* of validation-error objects (confirmed above), so\n95\t   `new Error(anArray)` stringifies it via `Array.prototype.toString` \u2192\n96\t   `[object Object]`. Pre-existing in `request()`, shared with `AddTask`'s\n97\t   create path, unchanged by this diff. `docs/design.md:64-71` reasons\n98\t   through this and accepts the asymmetry with AC4 as intentional parity\n99\t   with create's existing behavior, so it's correctly out of scope for this\n100\t   cycle \u2014 flagging only because AC5 says the rejection must be \"surfaced to\n101\t   the user,\" and today that surfacing is unreadable. Worth a follow-up\n102\t   ticket on `request()`'s error formatting.\n103\t2. **No frontend test framework exists** (`frontend/package.json` has no\n104\t   `test` script/deps). All frontend-side AC1-5 coverage in this cycle came\n105\t   from the e2e suite, not a component-level test. That's real coverage\n106\t   (exercises the actual rendered app), but there's no fast, isolated unit\n107\t   test for the edit-mode reducer logic. Worth a separate ticket to stand up\n108\t   frontend testing infra, as the story stage already flagged.\n109\t3. `backend/tests/_scratch_check_422.py` is untracked scratch content left\n110\t   in the working tree from an earlier stage. Not part of this diff's\n111\t   intended deliverable \u2014 worth deleting before commit, or folding its\n112\t   assertion into `backend/tests/test_tasks.py` as a permanent test (it\n113\t   currently duplicates coverage now also captured by\n114\t   `e2e/test_tasks_e2e.py::test_oversized_title_surfaces_an_unreadable_error`,\n115\t   just at the backend layer instead of end-to-end).\n116\t\n117\t## Spec/design conformance (for the record)\n118\t\n119\tAC6-8 (backend): verified by existing + re-run `backend/tests/test_tasks.py`\n120\ttests, all passing. AC1-4 (frontend): verified live via new e2e tests, all\n121\tpassing, functionally correct. AC5 (frontend): rejection itself works\n122\tcorrectly (title unchanged, save blocked) but the surfaced message is\n123\tunreadable \u2014 tracked as a non-blocking follow-up per design's own accepted\n124\tasymmetry. Both open questions (done tasks stay editable; single-edit-mode)\n125\timplemented as their stated defaults and verified live.\n126\t\n127\tThe one blocking defect is narrow and the fix is a one-line `aria-label`\n128\tchange in `TaskList.jsx`; everything else in the implementation is correct\n129\tand well-tested.\n130\t\n131\tVERDICT: blocked\n132\t", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01JSeSyKKatBy5kU8dzxcfcq", "session_id": "0eebde98-5c8c-4448-8749-7a27e4e34a84"}}
